      "title": "Geometry: The Pythagorean Theorem",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Finally, let's look at another cornerstone of geometry: the Pythagorean theorem. This famous theorem applies specifically to a special kind of triangle: the right-angled triangle. A right-angled triangle is any triangle that has one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees. The two sides that form the right angle are called the 'legs' of the triangle. The third side, which is always the longest and is opposite the right angle, is called the 'hypotenuse'. The Pythagorean theorem describes a beautiful and simple relationship between the lengths of these three sides. If we label the lengths of the two legs 'a' and 'b', and the length of the hypotenuse 'c', the theorem states that a-squared plus b-squared is equal to c-squared. In other words, if you take the length of each leg and multiply it by itself, and then add those two results together, you will get the exact same number as when you multiply the length of the hypotenuse by itself. This theorem is incredibly useful for finding a missing side length of a right triangle when you know the other two, and it forms the basis for much of trigonometry and distance calculations in coordinate geometry.",
